 * 【42†Index】\nL50:     * 【43†Search Videos】\nL51:   * 【0†Liberty Fund Network】\nL52:     * 【1†Econlib】\nL53:     * 【3†OLL†oll.libertyfund.org】\nL54:     * 【4†Adam Smith Works†www.adamsmithworks.org】\nL55:     * 【5†Library of Law & Liberty†lawliberty.org】\nL56:   * 【1†Home】  /\nL57: # Labor Econ Versus the World: Essays on the World's Greatest Market\nL58: \nL59: 【44†0】\nL60: ##### 【45†Bryan Caplan】\nL61: \nL62: Categories: 【46†Books: Reviews and Suggested Readings】 【47†Labor Market】\nL63: \nL64: By Bryan Caplan, Jan 24 2022\nL65: \nL66: 【48†Image】\nL67: \nL68: Big announcement: I’m publishing eight new books of my all-time best EconLog essays.  Today, the first volume – entitled 【49†Labor Econ Versus the World: Essays on the World’s Greatest Market†www.amazon.com】, goes on sale on Amazon.\nL69: I spent weeks reviewing the several thousand essays I’ve written since 2005, organizing them all by theme.  Labor Econ Versus the World collects my top pieces on the world of work, broken down into four categories.  “Laissez-Faire and Labor” analyzes the sorry effects of labor market regulation for workers and the rest of the economy.  “Open Borders” turns to the vast yet neglected gains of letting everyone on Earth work in whatever country they please.  “Education Without Romance” debunks the prevailing 【50†human capital】 model of education in favor of the signaling heresy.  “The Search for Success” shows how you can use the insights of labor economics to avoid poverty, advance your career, marry well, and optimize your parenting.\nL70: Can’t you just read all of these essays for free online?  Absolutely.  But Labor Econ Versus the World curates your experience.  Instead of sifting through almost two decades of blog posts, you can read one top piece after another.  Editor Jack Pfeffercorn has beautifully typeset the entire book.  And the book is priced to sell: Just $12 for the paperback, and a mere $9.99 for the e-book.  If you enjoy this blog, I’m confident that you’ll get ample consumer surplus from the purchase.\nL71: P.S. If you’re a journalist, influencer, or teacher considering course adoption, please email aachar3@gmu.edu for a review copy.\nL72: \nL73: P.P.S. I’m available for interviews and other media events for the book.  Just email me to set things up!\nL74: \nL75: Comments are closed.\nL76: #### RECENT POST\nL77: \nL78: Jan 24 2022\nL79: \nL80: Price Controls\nL81: \nL82: #### 【51†Matthews Interviews Henderson on Price Controls】\nL83: \nL84: ###### 【52†David Henderson】\nL85: \nL86: Last week health economist Merrill Matthews of the Institute for Policy Innovation (IPI), based in Dallas, read my Hoover article on price controls and, on that basis, interviewed me for about half an hour. Here's the interview. A few highlights: 7:28: Prices aren't arbitrary. 9:13: Can corporations charge ...\nL87: \nL88: 【53†3】\nL89: \nL90: 【51†Read More】\nL91: \nL92: Jan 24 2022\nL93: \nL94: Macroeconomics\nL95: #### 【54†No, \"the market\" isn't worried about \"the Fed\"】\nL96: \nL97: ###### 【55†Scott Sumner】\nL98: \nL99: Falling stock prices have recently been in the news. You see headlines about stock investors being worried about \"the Fed\". But when you read the actual articles there is often little or no discussion of how Fed policy might be depressing stock prices. More often, the claim is that markets are worried about rising inte...\nL100: \nL101: 【56†15】\nL102: \nL103: 【54†Read More】\nL104: \nL105: Jan 24 2022\nL106: \nL107: Books: Reviews and Suggested Readings\nL108: #### 【57†Labor Econ Versus the World: Essays on the Worl...】\nL109: \nL110: ###### 【45†Bryan Caplan】\nL111: \nL112: Big announcement: I'm publishing eight new books of my all-time best EconLog essays.  Today, the first volume - entitled Labor Econ Versus the World: Essays on the World's Greatest Market, goes on sale on Amazon. I spent weeks reviewing the several thousand essays I've written since 2005, organizing them all by th...\nL113: \nL114: 【44†0】\nL115: \nL116